  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1229 PM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nShowers and thunderstorms coverage continues to increase each \nday through Monday as the active monsoon plume remains over the \nCO and a well defined disturbance moves through southwest flow \nMonday afternoon and night.\n\nMonday still looks like the wettest day of the period, though \ntiming of the shortwave Monday night is not well juxtaposed with\npeak heating, which could reduce the risk of flash flooding. \nWill need to monitor the timing/position of a cold front as it \ndrops through the southeast plains Monday/Monday night. If the \nfront moves in during the day on Monday, east to southeast \nupslope flow may provide a better focus and sufficient \nconfidence in flash flooding for the Aspen Acres burn scar to \nwarrant a Flash Flood Watch. However, not all models agree with \nthis timing and details will need to be refined as we get \ncloser. \n\nTuesday looks drier behind the shortwave, though there is still\nsufficient moisture for isolated afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms. Then moisture gradually increases again as \nsouthwest flow picks up some Pacific moisture and spreads it \nacross southern CO through late week. Best precipitation chances\nappear focused across the mountains (mainly Continental Divide)\nwhere better moisture fetch will be in place. East of the \nmountains, moisture looks high based again, though at least some\nisolated thunderstorms should return to the forecast for most \nareas by Saturday.",
